<html>span.text1806618Font1 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101;}&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1806618Font1&quot;  &gt;In the XIAFLEX trials in Peyronie&apos;s disease (Study 1 and Study 2), 65.5% of XIAFLEX-treated patients developed penile hematoma and 14.5% developed penile ecchymosis. Patients with abnormal coagulation (except for patients taking low-dose aspirin, eg, â‰¤150 mg per day) were excluded from participating in these studies. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;</html>
<rtf>{\rtf1\ansi\deflang1033\ftnbj\uc1\deff1&#x0A;{\fonttbl{\f0 \froman \fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 \fswiss Arial;}}&#x0A;{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red255\green255\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue0 ;}&#x0A;{\stylesheet{\f1\fs24\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Normal;}{\cs1\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Default Paragraph Font;}{\s2\snext0\cf2\cb1\ulc2 ;}}&#x0A;{\*\revtbl{Unknown;}}&#x0A;\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\headery720\footery720\deftab360\formshade\aendnotes\aftnnrlc\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot&#x0A;\sectd\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1800\margrsxn1800\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1440\headery720\footery720\sbkpage\pgncont\pgndec&#x0A;\plain\plain\f1\fs24\ql\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 In the XIAFLEX trials in Peyronie&apos;s disease (Study 1 and Study 2), 65.5% of XIAFLEX-treated patients developed penile hematoma and 14.5% developed penile ecchymosis. Patients with abnormal coagulation (except &#x0A;for patients taking low-dose aspirin, eg, \u8804 \&apos;3f150 mg per day) were excluded from participating in these studies. \plain\f1\fs24\par}&#x0A;</rtf>
<raw>﻿In the XIAFLEX trials in Peyronie&apos;s disease (Study 1 and Study 2), 65.5% of XIAFLEX-treated patients developed penile hematoma and 14.5% developed penile ecchymosis. Patients with abnormal coagulation (except for patients taking low-dose aspirin, eg, ≤150 mg per day) were excluded from participating in these studies. </raw>
