<html>span.text1807697Font1 { font-size:21px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#000000; font-weight:bold;}&#x0A;&lt;p  style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1807697Font1&quot;  &gt;Safety Note&#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;</html>
<rtf>{\rtf1\ansi\deflang1033\ftnbj\uc1\deff1&#x0A;{\fonttbl{\f0 \froman \fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 \fswiss Arial;}}&#x0A;{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red255\green255\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue0 ;}&#x0A;{\stylesheet{\f1\fs24\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Normal;}{\cs1\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Default Paragraph Font;}}&#x0A;{\*\revtbl{Unknown;}}&#x0A;\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\headery720\footery720\deftab360\formshade\aendnotes\aftnnrlc\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot&#x0A;\sectd\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1800\margrsxn1800\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1440\headery720\footery720\sbkpage\pgncont\pgndec&#x0A;\plain\plain\f1\fs24\ql\cf2\fs32\b Safety Note\par\plain\f1\fs24\par}&#x0A;</rtf>
<raw>﻿Safety Note&#x0A;</raw>
<numoflines>2</numoflines>
<numpubbullets>0</numpubbullets>
<textpublishlang>Inherit</textpublishlang>
<marginsize>0</marginsize>
</text>
<text id="1807698" parent="1807692" visible="false" ontop="true" fixed="true" proportional="false" defaulttext="none">
<name>ann_text</name>
<lastupdated>1769699280</lastupdated>
<respprops>
<tabletportrait>
<text>
<possize>
<size>
<cx>472</cx>
<cy>355</cy>
</size>
<point>
<x>162</x>
<y>208</y>
</point>
</possize>
</text>
</tabletportrait>
</respprops>
<possize>
<point>
<x>212</x>
<y>213</y>
</point>
<size>
<cx>606</cx>
<cy>271</cy>
</size>
</possize>
<cssclasses>hideMe a_text</cssclasses>
<opacity>100</opacity>
<html>span.text1807698Font1 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101;}&#x0A;span.text1807698Font2 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101; font-style:italic;}&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1807698Font1&quot;  &gt;Clinicians may be concerned about the potential for immunogenicity with products that are derived from a bacterial source, such as XIAFLEX. During clinical studies in Dupuytren&apos;s contracture and Peyronie&apos;s disease, patients were tested at multiple time points for antibodies to the protein components of XIAFLEX (AUX-I and AUX-II). In the Peyronie&apos;s disease clinical studies (Studies 1 and 2), at 6 weeks after the first treatment cycle of XIAFLEX 0.58 mg, approximately 75% of patients had antibodies against AUX-I and approximately 55% of patients had antibodies against AUX-II. Six weeks after the eighth injection (fourth treatment cycle) of XIAFLEX, more than 99% of XIAFLEX-treated patients developed high titers of antibodies to both AUX-I and AUX-II. As explained further on the next page, AUX-I and AUX-II share some characteristics with human matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). Because of this similarity, it is a theoretical possibility that antibodies to AUX-I and AUX-II could interfere with human MMPs. However,&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1807698Font2&quot;  &gt; in vitro&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1807698Font1&quot;  &gt; studies have found no evidence of such interference. Furthermore, there is no clinical evidence of any safety concerns related to AUX-I and AUX-II antibodies interfering with the normal activity of human MMPs. &amp;nbsp;&#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;</html>
<rtf>{\rtf1\ansi\deflang1033\ftnbj\uc1\deff1&#x0A;{\fonttbl{\f0 \froman \fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 \fswiss Arial;}}&#x0A;{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red255\green255\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue0 ;}&#x0A;{\stylesheet{\f1\fs24\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Normal;}{\cs1\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Default Paragraph Font;}{\s2\snext0\cf2\cb1\ulc2 ;}}&#x0A;{\*\revtbl{Unknown;}}&#x0A;\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\headery720\footery720\deftab360\formshade\aendnotes\aftnnrlc\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot&#x0A;\sectd\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1800\margrsxn1800\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1440\headery720\footery720\sbkpage\pgncont\pgndec&#x0A;\plain\plain\f1\fs24\ql Clinicians may be concerned about the potential for immunogenicity with products that are derived from a bacterial source, such as XIAFLEX. During clinical studies in Dupuytren&apos;s contracture and Peyronie&apos;s disease, patients were &#x0A;tested at multiple time points for antibodies to the protein components of XIAFLEX (AUX-I and AUX-II). In the Peyronie&apos;s disease clinical studies (Studies 1 and 2), at 6 weeks after the first treatment cycle of XIAFLEX 0.58 mg, approximately 75% of patients &#x0A;had antibodies against AUX-I and approximately 55% of patients had antibodies against AUX-II. Six weeks after the eighth injection (fourth treatment cycle) of XIAFLEX, more than 99% of XIAFLEX-treated patients developed high titers of antibodies to both &#x0A;AUX-I and AUX-II. As explained further on the next page, AUX-I and AUX-II share some characteristics with human matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). Because of this similarity, it is a theoretical possibility that antibodies to AUX-I and AUX-II could interfere &#x0A;with human MMPs. However,\i  in vitro\plain\f1\fs24  studies have found no evidence of such interference. Furthermore, there is no clinical evidence of any safety concerns related to AUX-I and AUX-II antibodies interfering with the normal activity of &#x0A;human MMPs.  \par}&#x0A;</rtf>
<raw>﻿Clinicians may be concerned about the potential for immunogenicity with products that are derived from a bacterial source, such as XIAFLEX. During clinical studies in Dupuytren&apos;s contracture and Peyronie&apos;s disease, patients were tested at multiple time points for antibodies to the protein components of XIAFLEX (AUX-I and AUX-II). In the Peyronie&apos;s disease clinical studies (Studies 1 and 2), at 6 weeks after the first treatment cycle of XIAFLEX 0.58 mg, approximately 75% of patients had antibodies against AUX-I and approximately 55% of patients had antibodies against AUX-II. Six weeks after the eighth injection (fourth treatment cycle) of XIAFLEX, more than 99% of XIAFLEX-treated patients developed high titers of antibodies to both AUX-I and AUX-II. As explained further on the next page, AUX-I and AUX-II share some characteristics with human matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). Because of this similarity, it is a theoretical possibility that antibodies to AUX-I and AUX-II could interfere with human MMPs. However, in vitro studies have found no evidence of such interference. Furthermore, there is no clinical evidence of any safety concerns related to AUX-I and AUX-II antibodies interfering with the normal activity of human MMPs.  </raw>
