<text id="1808312" parent="1808306" visible="false" ontop="true" fixed="true" proportional="false" publishasimage="true" defaulttext="none">
<name>ann_text</name>
<lastupdated>1769699459</lastupdated>
<respprops>
<tabletportrait>
<text>
<possize>
<size>
<cx>460</cx>
<cy>180</cy>
</size>
</possize>
</text>
</tabletportrait>
</respprops>
<possize>
<point>
<x>212</x>
<y>212</y>
</point>
<size>
<cx>603</cx>
<cy>181</cy>
</size>
</possize>
<cssclasses>hideMe a_text</cssclasses>
<opacity>100</opacity>
<html>span.text1808312Font1 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101;}&#x0A;span.text1808312Font2 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101; font-style:italic;}&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font1&quot;  &gt;The mechanism of action of XIAFLEX involves 2 highly selective microbial collagenases (AUX-I and AUX-II) that work together to disrupt types I and III collagen of Peyronie&apos;s plaques without damage to the nerves, arteries, or large veins, which contain type IV collagen. Disruption of the plaques reduces penile curvature. However, as described on the next page of the PI, there are no clinical data regarding the relative contributions of the individual collagenases (AUX-I or AUX-II) to the efficacy of XIAFLEX in the treatment of Peyronie&apos;s disease. In &lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font2&quot;  &gt;in vitro&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font1&quot;  &gt; studies, at higher doses and longer incubation times, nonfibrillar type IV collagen was affected, causing collagen lysis in small veins. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;</html>
<rtf>{\rtf1\ansi\deflang1033\ftnbj\uc1\deff1&#x0A;{\fonttbl{\f0 \froman \fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 \fswiss Arial;}}&#x0A;{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red255\green255\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue0 ;}&#x0A;{\stylesheet{\f1\fs24\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Normal;}{\cs1\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Default Paragraph Font;}{\s2\snext0\cf2\cb1\ulc2 ;}}&#x0A;{\*\revtbl{Unknown;}}&#x0A;\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\headery720\footery720\deftab360\formshade\aendnotes\aftnnrlc\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot&#x0A;\sectd\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1800\margrsxn1800\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1440\headery720\footery720\sbkpage\pgncont\pgndec&#x0A;\plain\plain\f1\fs24\ql The mechanism of action of XIAFLEX involves 2 highly selective microbial collagenases (AUX-I and AUX-II) that work together to disrupt types I and III collagen of Peyronie&apos;s plaques without damage to the nerves, arteries, or large &#x0A;veins, which contain type IV collagen. Disruption of the plaques reduces penile curvature. However, as described on the next page of the PI, there are no clinical data regarding the relative contributions of the individual collagenases (AUX-I or AUX-II) &#x0A;to the efficacy of XIAFLEX in the treatment of Peyronie&apos;s disease. In \i in vitro\plain\f1\fs24  studies, at higher doses and longer incubation times, nonfibrillar type IV collagen was affected, causing collagen lysis in small veins. \par}&#x0A;</rtf>
<raw>﻿The mechanism of action of XIAFLEX involves 2 highly selective microbial collagenases (AUX-I and AUX-II) that work together to disrupt types I and III collagen of Peyronie&apos;s plaques without damage to the nerves, arteries, or large veins, which contain type IV collagen. Disruption of the plaques reduces penile curvature. However, as described on the next page of the PI, there are no clinical data regarding the relative contributions of the individual collagenases (AUX-I or AUX-II) to the efficacy of XIAFLEX in the treatment of Peyronie&apos;s disease. In in vitro studies, at higher doses and longer incubation times, nonfibrillar type IV collagen was affected, causing collagen lysis in small veins. </raw>
<numoflines>9</numoflines>
<numpubbullets>0</numpubbullets>
<textpublishlang>Inherit</textpublishlang>
<marginsize>0</marginsize>
</text>
